#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186063972 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064173 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "630a6635-aac0-5627-82c8-8072b74dd8f3" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Intersection of Two Intervals Given Graphically"}] :question/id #uuid "02afcfcd-c405-5add-a291-75e6db923e3f" :question/math-academy-id "e-13943" :question/problem "What is the intersection of the intervals shown below, expressed as a number line?\n\n![](images/e2/e2da9ff989c1cee3365f88b4ba7bc8dba9ef31f20dc49ae8e4f87a79243473b0.png)" :question/worked-solution "The intersection of two intervals consists of the values that are in both intervals. So, it can be graphed by finding where the two intervals overlap:\n\n![](images/43/4333901508bff833b854769feabb729b836cdb3aae9827b5170546b2ffbe7d35.png)\n\nTherefore, the number line that shows the common set of values is:\n\n![](images/f9/f9d3a9e1da4be61938e41eb5ebe308ed85cd1f60f4fd29f211b2f72071afe159.png)\n\n**Watch out!** The point $-1$ is not included in the intersection since it does not lie in the second interval."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186063974 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064174 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4c60e9f3-3322-57c7-85da-e65ea232ef9e" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Intersection of Two Intervals"}] :question/id #uuid "669f96c6-1505-5b02-88aa-c99af18b24d7" :question/math-academy-id "e-13950" :question/problem "Find the intersection of intervals $(-\\infty, 3] \\cap (0, \\infty)$. Express the result as an inequality." :question/worked-solution "We can start by sketching the two intervals $(-\\infty, 3]$ and $(0, \\infty)$ separately:\n\n![](images/25/255da904e7d271006cef6a272379b8f160fc6cc9e5f8ede39d91141aea2eff7b.png)\n\n![](images/31/31ad28b45aa2cefa4d83cc06710e9ae81446809ec0ac42da5baae36e235e35a9.png)\n\nThe intersection $(-\\infty, 3] \\cap (0, \\infty)$ represents the values that are in both intervals. So, it can be graphed by finding where the two intervals overlap:\n\n![](images/6a/6a92713b9964c9b1583622553a7cdb3c9c700ecad95cc0581f1538d18d1fa42a.png)\n\nThus,\n\n$$\n(-\\infty, 3]\\cap(0, \\infty)={\\color{blue}(0,3]}\n$$\n\nThis corresponds to the inequality\n\n$$\n0 < x \\leq 3\n$$\n\n**Watch out!** The point $0$ is not included in the intersection since it does not lie in the second interval. On the other hand, the point $3$ is included in the intersection because it lies in both intervals."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186063976 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064175 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "85687901-9285-5cd2-a5f2-24299bd7d3d5" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Intersection of Two Intervals Given as Inequalities"}] :question/id #uuid "5aa36af9-33b3-58a4-b1ce-297f170faa44" :question/math-academy-id "e-13954" :question/problem "Given the inequalities $x < 3$ and $x \\leq -1$, express their common set of solutions as an interval." :question/worked-solution "We start by sketching the solutions to the two inequalities separately:\n\n![](images/16/166ce99c0ceedf97d38066b544b5dc6ff7ca63296572d952536193a83a410d94.png)\n\n![](images/7f/7ff88166ac6f9e631cf8928e91c1c299986893051013f15387e153ecf4237866.png)\n\nThe common set of values consists of the values that are in both intervals. So, it can be graphed by finding where the two intervals overlap:\n\n![](images/a7/a77508881158e793810249877b3941fe52ae9e04056813d2222daae4aa0d5e50.png)\n\nTherefore, the common set of values is\n${\\color{blue}(-\\infty, -1]}$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186063977 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064176 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d916584b-c94e-557f-a438-f0464c1cea87" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Intersection of Three Intervals"}] :question/id #uuid "db91e510-bd32-5272-be39-c5015758c81b" :question/math-academy-id "e-13958" :question/problem "Given the inequalities $x < 1$, $x \\geq 0$, and $-2 < x < 4$, express their common set of solutions as an interval." :question/worked-solution "To find the common set of values, we graph the inequalities on a number line and find where they overlap:\n\n![](images/25/2544eed03d4349fdd897e62447a9566843c53c7ab24001525d5148dba14fecd4.png)\n\nTherefore, the common set of values is\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}\\left[0, 1\\right)}\n$$\n\n**Watch out!** The point $0$ is included in the intersection since it lies in all three intervals. On the other hand, the point $1$ is not included in the intersection because it does not lie in the first interval."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064469 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064175 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "85687901-9285-5cd2-a5f2-24299bd7d3d5" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Intersection of Two Intervals Given as Inequalities"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9ec9ccc6-fbd8-55a2-bf47-79543bcbd946" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[2, 3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186061045} {:answer/id #uuid "c57ad88c-4f0e-542e-8a98-6c4fc465958a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2, 3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186061046} {:answer/id #uuid "cf2b92e2-59d3-5bfe-8278-f50bc02b053b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2, 3]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186061047} {:answer/id #uuid "ffce6709-9460-53dd-84d1-a5041cddffe2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[3, 5)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186061048} {:answer/id #uuid "0adaf8a9-6cfa-51a8-9b87-4e71eab9c7b0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(3, 5]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186061049}] :answer-field/id #uuid "6e701a6d-6b18-569a-8eea-54880c65a3f3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186061044}] :question/id #uuid "f46011a6-2365-5214-8b00-ddd367e7ff91" :question/math-academy-id "q-102006" :question/problem "Given the inequalities $3 < x < 7$ and $2 \\le x \\le 5$, express their common set of solutions as an interval."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064669 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064176 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d916584b-c94e-557f-a438-f0464c1cea87" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Intersection of Three Intervals"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8c97f244-2337-5d7b-b7ac-2839c7c67184" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(6, 3]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186062072} {:answer/id #uuid "48f6b3fd-c78e-59a0-bbdd-b7855d1e0adc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-6, ∞)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186062073} {:answer/id #uuid "4ab9ff11-54cd-55db-b4b2-c880fe9d08fb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-3, 5)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186062074} {:answer/id #uuid "5445bd59-59ca-5468-bfce-197b70079e91" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-3, 3]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186062075} {:answer/id #uuid "77e61861-0e77-5dcb-a110-812be2a84d1a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(- ∞, 3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186062076}] :answer-field/id #uuid "ae653d46-ca6d-5a93-abdb-d21333d9e21d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186062071}] :question/id #uuid "fcd15d2f-86ee-5a4a-83a6-5df06fb72031" :question/math-academy-id "q-25894" :question/problem "Given the inequalities $x \\ge - 6$, $-3 < x < 5$, and $x \\le 3$, express their common set of solutions as an interval."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064702 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064176 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d916584b-c94e-557f-a438-f0464c1cea87" :knowledg